[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me solve this. This is a classic result. The Whitney embedding theorem [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstates that any smooth n-manifold can be embedded in R^{2n}. But here we have a [0m[38;2;124;124;124m*topological* manifold (not necessarily smooth). The question is about [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopological manifolds.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor topological manifolds, the embedding theorem is also true. Every compact [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopological n-manifold embeds in some R^N. This follows from results by Menger-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mNöbeling (every compact n-dimensional metric space embeds in R^{2n+1}) and the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfact that topological manifolds are metrizable and have covering dimension n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be careful. The Menger-Nöbeling theorem states that every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact metric space of (covering) dimension n embeds in R^{2n+1}. A compact [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtopological n-manifold is a compact metric space (since it's second-countable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHausdorff, hence metrizable by Urysohn, and compact) of covering dimension n.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is yes, every compact topological n-manifold embeds in R^{2n+1}.[0m

