  $Q=[0,1]^\omega$ (every compact metric space embeds in $Q$). Triangulate $Q$
  by finer and finer cubical subdivisions. Using the definition of covering
  dimension $\le n$, one shows that for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a
  continuous map $f_\varepsilon : X \to Q$ with $|f_\varepsilon - \iota|[3m\infty <[0m
  [3m\varepsilon$ whose image lies in the $(n)$-skeleton of the subdivision — i.e.,[0m
  [3m$f[0m\varepsilon$ is $\varepsilon$-close to the inclusion $\iota:X\hookrightarrow
  Q$ and "misses" all cells of dimension $\ge n+1$ in a suitable sense.
  [38;2;124;124;124m2. [0m[1mGeneral position / Baire category argument.[0m Consider the space $C(X,
  \mathbb{R}^{2n+1})$ of continuous maps with the uniform topology. For each
  pair of distinct points $x\ne y$ in $X$, the set $$A_{x,y} = {g : g(x)\ne
  g(y)}$$ is open and dense (density follows from the fact that the "diagonal-
  avoidance" condition is generic when the target dimension exceeds $2n$).
  Likewise, for the self-intersection set, requiring $g$ to be injective is
  expressible as a countable intersection of open dense sets using a countable
  dense subset of $X\times X\setminus\Delta$. The crucial dimension count: a map
  $X\to\mathbb{R}^{2n+1}$ can be perturbed to avoid the diagonal in $X\times X$
  because $\dim(X\times X)\le 2n < 2n+1$, so by the [1mHurewicz theorem[0m on
  extending maps while avoiding a set of lower dimension, a generic perturbation
  is injective.More concretely: the set of embeddings is a dense $G_\delta$ in
  $C(X,\mathbb{R}^{2n+1})$ whenever $\dim X \le n$ and the target has dimension
  $\ge 2n+1$.
  [38;2;124;124;124m3. [0m[1mExistence.[0m Since $C(X,\mathbb{R}^{2n+1})$ is a complete metric space
  (uniform topology, $X$ compact), the Baire category theorem guarantees that
  the intersection of these countably many open dense sets is non-empty. Hence
  an embedding $X\hookrightarrow\mathbb{R}^{2n+1}$ exists.

